+### 16.9 Proposed LIB-5 boundary and invariants (v9 draft)
+
+**Proposal only; requires Justin's review and specification merge.** This amendment schedules a bounded private exchange protocol for independent library nodes. It authorizes no implementation, peer enrollment, contributor-data transfer, production change, training, model release or public spectral release. I1–I15, L1–L7 and B1–B8 remain binding. Its primary sources are the existing measurement contract (§16.1–16.2), untrusted local-node boundary (§16.3), hosted grant/identity/withdrawal rules (§16.5–16.8), photo gate (§11, §15) and governance guardrails. The limits below are proposed engineering bounds, not measured capacity or an anonymity guarantee.
+
+A **library namespace** (`node_id` in LIB-1/4) groups samples and is bound to one contributor in hosted intake. A **federation peer** is a separately enrolled, human-operated installation with an opaque peer UUID and narrowly approved authority. A namespace, a phone-generated UUID, a network address or successful schema validation is not a peer credential. Forking and running the open local tools remains free; admission to another operator's private records is a separate opt-in decision and cannot be bought under B2/B5.
+
+The proposal adds these constraints:
+
+- Smartphone observations remain the capture/sample-linking entry point. `camera_rgb` remains uncalibrated three-channel imagery; `instrument_reflectance` retains explicit wavelengths, fractional reflectance, uncertainty and declared instrument/calibration identity. An exchange receipt supplies no physical pairing, instrument-origin, laboratory or biological verification; those remain LIB-6 work.
+- Every real transfer requires a separately reviewed recipient-specific scope grounded in the original contributor's immutable acceptance. Existing photo-publication terms are not assumed to authorize private peer sharing. A later grant, actor, node or recipient cannot revive an original withdrawn sample or measurement.
+- Peer identity and permissions are server/operator-derived, scoped, expiring, revocable and rate-limited per human of record. No global Supabase service-role key or contributor session is delegated to a peer or agent. Secrets stay in the existing GitHub/Supabase secret stores and never in bundles, node files or CLI arguments; an alternative peer secret-store arrangement requires a separate owner decision. Public agent REST/MCP remains anonymous, read-only and unchanged (I12). Private node tools are human-operated research tools, not a new private agent interface.
+- Transport contains no precise location, raw image/upload bytes, original instrument file, exact timestamp, instrument serial, person/auth identity, private path, external URL, free-text label or credential. The permitted scientific payload is still private: removing those fields or changing identifiers is not a spectral-fingerprinting/privacy clearance under L6.
+- All imported evidence is initially unverified and unavailable to field training, tuning, ordinary holdout evaluation, map aggregation and publication. Caller rights/QA/holdout/provenance claims never become authoritative. Existing photo screening, synthetic partitions, sealed restrictions, QA and original-license checks remain effective.
+- Withdrawal wins over pending, delayed and repeated declarations. Minimal opaque refusal identities survive payload erasure. Offline or unmanaged copies cannot be claimed erased immediately; unresolved recipients remain reported as pending rather than completed.
+
+### 16.10 Proposed private event contract and local preparation
+
+LIB-5A defines `gsip-node-exchange-v1` as strict UTF-8 JSON, not a raw `gsip-library-v1` record and not an archive containing arbitrary files. Each event is at most 2,000,000 input bytes, with nesting at most 12, no duplicate/unknown keys or non-finite numbers, canonical case-insensitive UUID text normalized for comparison, and strict integer sequences from 0 through 9,007,199,254,740,991. No URL is dereferenced and no credential or external host is read by the parser. These are proposed version-one bounds; changing them requires a reviewed contract version.
+
+Every event has exactly `schema_version`, `event_id`, `source_peer_id`, `destination_peer_id`, `scope_id`, `stream_sequence`, `previous_event_sha256`, `kind` and `payload`. IDs are opaque UUIDs. `schema_version` is exactly `gsip-node-exchange-v1`; `kind` is `declare` or `retire`. A stream is exactly the normalized `(source_peer_id, destination_peer_id, scope_id)` tuple. Its first sequence is zero with a null previous digest; subsequent events reference the preceding source-ledger event's lowercase 64-hex SHA-256. Each sequence slot has one immutable event identity and exact-byte digest; reuse with different identity or bytes is a conflict. Each event identity also belongs to one stream and slot. The digest covers the exact UTF-8 bytes. This is an integrity/idempotency convention, not a signature or sender authentication. Do not claim cross-language canonical JSON equivalence; retries retain the original bytes. Changed spelling, whitespace or number representation conflicts with an existing event identity.
+
+The receiver advances its committed sequence/digest head only over consecutive, validated slots whose previous digest matches that head. Gapped declarations remain quarantined and confer no use authority. An authenticated retirement applies its monotonic refusal immediately, independently of chain commitment, and never skips or rewrites the committed head. Its slot can commit later once its predecessors validate; later declarations resume only after all intervening slots validate and current rights still allow them. A late declaration covered by a refusal is never stored as scientific payload; any permitted in-memory chain validation does not resurrect it. A conflict holds normal stream processing while retirement still restricts use. An irrecoverable gap remains held; version one has no automatic reset, fabricated predecessor or caller-controlled new-scope escape.
+
+A `declare` payload has exactly `sample_handle`, `measurement_handle`, `protocol_version`, `context`, `measurement` and `synthetic_claim`. Handles are recipient-and-scope-specific UUID aliases, allocated once in a private origin ledger and retained across retries; they are not replacement source identities. Protocol/context and the discriminated measurement obey §16.2's existing bounds and units. `synthetic_claim` is a strict boolean: true adds a permanent synthetic restriction, while false supplies no field-data verification. Labels and caller-supplied `grant_id`, acceptance, consent/QA/holdout/verification/provenance fields are refused, not silently stripped. Instrument hashes and IDs remain declarations and never authorize cross-contributor matching.
+
+A `retire` payload has exactly `sample_handle` and `measurement_handles`, a unique list of zero through 100 opaque UUID aliases. Retirement is sample-wide; the list supplies additional known measurement refusals, never limits the sample's coverage. Minimal tombstones contain only approved stream/scope/source-alias identity and generic retirement state, with no scientific payload, source hash, context, reviewer, credential or location. A missing earlier declaration cannot bypass an authenticated refusal: remember the tombstone before processing any delayed payload. Authentication of retirement after normal scope/actor revocation requires the separately approved cleanup authority in §16.13; a retired contributor's old token is never revived to send it.
+
+Covered retirement erases full declaration-event bytes and derived scientific/context payload. The synthetic simulator may retain private sequence-slot and exact-event digest metadata separately from minimal refusal tombstones to demonstrate conflicts and chain behavior. Such digests are derived private integrity records, not anonymity or proof of complete remote erasure. Their purpose, retention and erasure behavior with source-lineage records require the explicit LIB-5C privacy/legal decision before real data; a synthetic cleanup acknowledgment proves only the absence of covered local payload.
+
+LIB-5A can implement only pure validation, exact-byte digest/idempotency helpers and synthetic conformance fixtures after this specification is approved. LIB-5B can then implement a private draft/outbox/recipient-ledger simulator, using mode-0700 directories, mode-0600 files, non-following file reads, bounded regular single-link files, atomic writes and the existing local locking discipline. It requires an operator-designated private allowed root and rejects traversal, symlinks, special files, multi-link payloads and conflicting identities. Shared/public/cloud folders are prohibited by operator policy; the implementation cannot claim exhaustive detection of every sync provider. It makes no HTTP call, opens no listener, reads no secret, automatically scans no contributor directory, and supplies no field-data export. Synthetic fixtures are labeled and local; local completion establishes protocol behavior only.
+
+### 16.11 Proposed source authority, deduplication and scientific boundaries
+
+Existing node CLIs remain filesystem-only and never upload or publish. Any real transport is a separately approved trusted service adapter, not an expansion of the public agent tools. LIB-5C must verify an enrolled live human-operated peer and an active exact source/destination scope before any real read or mutation. The source authority derives original contributor/grant, immutable terms and license acceptance, source sample/measurement identity, current rights, QA, synthetic and sealed restrictions from trusted state; it accepts none as caller authority. Source and receiver keep immutable event digests, their own opaque lineage mapping and private receipts. Network authentication proves an allowed sender, not the truth of its instrument, calibration, physical sample or laboratory claim. No peer may self-assign provenance tiers 2/3, certify a gold label or open a publication gate.
+
+Version one deduplicates exact received event/measurement identities and known lineage aliases only within the same original contributor/human of record. A verified source mapping to the same original sample keeps all copies/repeated measurements in one conservative lineage group, with one training partition and sample-wide withdrawal/synthetic/holdout restrictions. It does not increase independent sample, observation or contributor counts. A digest match detects a claimed byte duplicate; it does not prove the same physical material. Unresolved pairing is labeled unverified and held out of field preparation until LIB-6 verifies the physical linkage.
+
+There is no global hash index, public membership query, spectral-similarity matching or cross-contributor sample join. A peer cannot impose a restriction on another contributor using a claimed source digest. Different contributors claiming the same physical material require the separate policy/privacy decision in §16.13; the version-one default refuses that linkage. Recipient-specific handles reduce explicit identifier reuse, but the protocol makes no claim that scientific arrays or private copies are anonymous or unlinkable. Individual spectral/public lineage release remains LIB-7, gated by a measured fingerprinting/linkage/generalization review under L6/I7.
+
+Known sealed or withdrawn source samples cannot enter a normal exchange payload. A source returns a generic unavailable result without disclosing a holdout label, seal, reason or hidden evaluation membership. Incoming declarations can only add restrictions; loss of a record, grant, label or alias cannot unseal a known lineage group. Receipt/bundle content never grants training eligibility. No original-file storage, laboratory/biological assay ingestion, automatic training, reconstruction service, map estimator or model-weight publication is introduced by LIB-5.
+
+### 16.12 Proposed rights propagation, bounds and honest completion
+
+Source withdrawal invalidates outstanding transfer permissions under the canonical contributor-first lock before later declarations can be admitted. The source records minimal refusal identities and a durable private cleanup outbox before deleting covered payload. Hosted integration must preserve the existing rights-worker signatures/bodies, add honest completion checks and prove existing-data compatibility; production migration remains a separate exact-target gate. Source, namespace, measurement and event UUID changes cannot evade existing source/grant refusals.
+
+A receiver checks current authority again before accepting a declaration and before any separately authorized downstream use. Unknown, expired, disconnected or unavailable source authority holds the data; cached positive receipts do not authorize it. Declarations with a gap are held; duplicate identical events are idempotent; conflicts stop the stream. Valid retirement is applied promptly even if earlier declarations are missing, and subsequent arrival cannot recreate payload. A cleanup acknowledgment records the exact retired scope/alias set and absence of covered private payload, not merely receipt of the event. An already-withdrawn grant cannot be exchanged as a fresh sample under a later grant.
+
+Version one proposes at most 100 newly accepted declaration events per human of record per UTC day across its scopes, with exact retries consuming no new admission. The recipient has a separately enforced aggregate capacity; all limits are checked before work is claimed, with no upstream lookup in this protocol. Cleanup has priority over declaration queues and is not denied by a spent contribution allowance; its own bounded claims still prevent starvation/abuse. A local batch processes at most 20 events, uses at most five transient attempts with 60/120/240/480-second backoff, and holds terminal/conflicting work for private operator attention. These are initial proposal values, not a throughput or deletion-SLA measurement.
+
+The synthetic recipient has explicit aggregate limits: 100 enrolled scopes, 1,000 queued declaration events, 20 gapped events per stream, 10,000 retained sequence slots, 10,000 sample/measurement refusal identities, 1,000 pending cleanup items and 200,000,000 total queued payload bytes. A measurement handle counts separately toward refusal capacity. Limits apply across all humans/streams in that recipient, not per caller-chosen UUID. Admission reserves capacity atomically before durable writes; rejected work creates no partial payload. Each enrolled scope reserves one durable refusal-fence slot before any declaration is admitted. Neither retries nor new event/scope IDs bypass aggregate capacity.
+
+At declaration/gap/slot/payload capacity, refuse new declarations generically and preserve existing data and refusal state. Cleanup coalesces by enrolled scope/sample, remains independent of the daily contribution quota, and uses bounded claims. If a retirement cannot retain its bounded alias/item/slot records, atomically fence its already enrolled scope before doing further work; all declarations and downstream use in that scope then stay held. Keep the erasure obligation pending and acknowledge only after covered payload absence is verified. Never evict a tombstone, advance a fabricated chain head or reopen a fenced scope to make space. Unknown scopes gain no admission or mutation authority; no real cleanup-authentication mechanism is selected here. Scope-fence release, retention changes or gap recovery beyond validated predecessors require the later reviewed LIB-5C protocol. These simulator bounds and conservative saturation rules are provisional and require operational review before any real rollout.
+
+Recipient erasure covers private declaration/context/measurement payload, known aliases, derived local preparation files and pending drafts; durable refusals remain. Unreachable or unmanaged copies, previously released immutable datasets and models are recorded honestly. The protocol cannot atomically commit a database decision with another node's filesystem or network transfer. It asserts no instantaneous distributed erasure, delivery or useful scientific adoption. Existing 30-day deletion commitments are not extended: missing acknowledgments are an unresolved obligation and require the approved operational response, not a fabricated completion flag.
+
+### 16.13 Owner decisions, assumptions and completion evidence
+
+These are new choices, not approvals inferred from existing grants or this draft. No real-data peer adapter or transport is implemented before Justin resolves them; counsel/scientific/privacy review remains required where indicated.
+
+| Choice                                         | Recommended version-one policy                                                                                                                                                                                                    | Alternative requiring separate review                                                                                                |
+| ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| Peer admission and accountability              | Owner-enrolled organizations/people with a named private human of record; anyone can still fork/use the local code.                                                                                                               | Automatic enrollment or discovery: define abuse, removal and consent controls first.                                                 |
+| Recipient-specific private sharing consent     | Keep real peer sharing closed until counsel approves a specific opt-in scope, purpose, retention and recipient notice; no assumption that publication consent covers it.                                                          | Start with already released public datasets only; this is public consumption, not private federation.                                |
+| Scoped credential and cleanup authority        | A reviewed expiring/revocable delegated credential backed by approved secret stores, plus narrowly authenticated steward cleanup after ordinary scope revocation. Select the exact mechanism before LIB-5C.                       | A signed manifest or certificate scheme, with key lifecycle/dependency/license/size review first; schema/hash alone is insufficient. |
+| Same-material claims by different contributors | Refuse cross-contributor linkage in version one; retain only within-owner verified origin aliases and unverified pairing status.                                                                                                  | Private manual joint-consent linkage after privacy/scientific review; no public linkage or similarity search by default.             |
+| Recipient duties and loss of contact           | Require explicit erasure/derived-file duties and private acknowledgment; disconnected authority holds use and unresolved copies remain reported. Approve private event-digest/lineage retention and erasure before real exchange. | A stronger distributed fence or different offline-use policy needs a new specification and evidence.                                 |
+
+**Explicit assumptions:** (1) private peer exchange is a new purpose not yet authorized by the current legal drafts; (2) a federation peer is distinct from a phone/library namespace; (3) available software can validate bytes and lineage without verifying real physical material; (4) no network/key mechanism, measured-spectrum privacy clearance, reference-data license or lab adapter is selected by this proposal; (5) the proposed quotas/size/retry values require operational measurement before rollout; (6) library fixtures remain synthetic and code/CI success supplies no evidence of a useful field model or soil-biome finding.
+
+Acceptance evidence is separate per package. LIB-5A proves strict schemas, resource bounds, stable retries, conflicts and forbidden authority fields with synthetic TS/Python conformance tests. LIB-5B proves private filesystem handling, tombstones-before-erasure, delayed/duplicate/conflicting events, alias restrictions, queue bounds and disconnection holds with a fake authority/transport that never performs network I/O. After owner decisions, LIB-5C needs additive role/migration tests, real two-connection rights races in both orders and partial-lock boundaries, scoped credential revocation, recipient/source isolation, honest old-worker completion and end-to-end synthetic peer cleanup. Every package preserves public anonymous reads and existing app/capture/photo/holdout gates; each new dependency needs its license, maintenance signal and size before adoption. No code merge, green check or receipt authorizes production peer enrollment or private/public data transfer.
+
